<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Afaq Khoja</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1626">1626</a>–<a href="/years/1694">1694</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Hidayat Allah</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Afaq Khoja (1626–1694), also called Hidayat <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allah">Allah</a>, was a Naqshbandi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> master and leader of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aqtaghliq">Aqtaghliq</a> (White Mountain) faction of khojas in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashgaria">Kashgaria</a>. Expelled by the khan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yarkent-khanate">Yarkent</a>, he sought help from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/5th-dalai-lama">Fifth Dalai Lama</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dzungars">Dzungar</a> ruler Galdan, whose conquest of 1680 installed him as ruler of the Tarim oases under Dzungar overlordship. His mausoleum near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashgar">Kashgar</a> is a major pilgrimage site.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/afaq-khoja.jpg" alt="Afaq Khoja" width="320" height="236" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Colegota · CC BY-SA 2.5 es · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Afaq_Khoja_Mausoleum_in_Kashgar,_11_October_2005.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
